<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-20969] PCC-RW: disable functionality by default for 2.18.0</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20969</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;With PCC-RO becoming generally available for 2.18.0, there is a risk that users will also consider PCC-RW as ready for production and use it on their systems. The current PCC-RW functionality is only a proof-of-concept, and poses significant risk to user data because it moves the *&lt;b&gt;only copy&lt;/b&gt;* of the data into a single client-side cache/archive (like an hsm-released archive file).  If the client becomes inaccessibe or the "cache" filesystem is cleaned up or lost, then the only copy of the file data is lost with it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In order for PCC-RW to become suitable for production use, the HSM archive copy would need to become an FLR mirror copy (&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-13637" title="Combining RO-PCC/RW-PCC/HSM with FLR HLD" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-13637"&gt;LU-13637&lt;/a&gt;) so that the main file copy is not released from the filesystem when it is copied into the client PCC-RW archive, along with other bug fixes, testing, etc. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add a configuration parameter to disable the PCC-RW functionality by default in the 2.18.0 release, which is enabled during testing in &lt;tt&gt;sanity-pcc.sh&lt;/tt&gt; (and possibly other scripts when needed), with a clear message about how to enable it again, as was done for FKR-ECRO and CSDC while they are under development.  Add an environment variable check in &lt;tt&gt;lctl&lt;/tt&gt;/&lt;tt&gt;lfs&lt;/tt&gt; that causes PCC-RW commands to fail with a clear user message as the front line, and a tunable &lt;tt&gt;llite.&amp;#42;.enable_pcc_rw&lt;/tt&gt; parameter on the client and &lt;tt&gt;nodemap.&amp;#42;.enable_pcc_rw&lt;/tt&gt; RBAC (or &lt;tt&gt;mdt.&amp;#42;.enable_pcc_rw&lt;/tt&gt; parameter) on the server (including the &lt;tt&gt;default&lt;/tt&gt; nodemap) that prints a self-referential message on the console about how to enable it so that it can be configured on a per-nodemap basis. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We may as well add similar &lt;tt&gt;&amp;#42;.&amp;#42;.enable_pcc_ro&lt;/tt&gt; parameters at the same time, but enabled by default for this release.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
